---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Methodology — TN Assembly Watch" current="methodology">
  <article class="prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert max-w-3xl">
    <h1>Methodology</h1>

    <p>
      This dashboard is a public, post-EOD summary of Tamil Nadu Legislative Assembly sittings.
      It is independent, non-partisan, and every derived claim on the site links back to the
      exact minute of the source video and to the underlying JSON in our open data repository.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Video and audio</strong> — the assembly's own YouTube broadcast.</li>
      <li><strong>Business list, questions, bills</strong> — official proceedings published by <code>assembly.tn.gov.in</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Speaker attribution and per-minute timing</strong> — derived via automated speech recognition
        (Whisper large-v3) and speaker diarization (pyannote.audio), cross-referenced against the
        official speaker list for the sitting.</li>
      <li><strong>Content classification and topic tags</strong> — assigned by a large language model
        (Claude Haiku 4.5) applied to each segment against the rubric below. Every classification
        is stamped with a confidence score.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Content classification rubric (draft — v0)</h2>
    <p>Every segment is placed in exactly one of these buckets.</p>

    <dl class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 not-prose">
      <div class="border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 rounded p-4">
        <dt class="font-semibold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400">Substantive</dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 mt-1">
          Discussion of policy, facts, budget figures, laws, or specific administrative matters.
          Ministerial answers with concrete data. Questions demanding accountability.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 rounded p-4">
        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">Procedural</dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 mt-1">
          Motions, points of order, laying of papers, Speaker's rulings, adjournments,
          voting formalities.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 rounded p-4">
        <dt class="font-semibold text-pink-700 dark:text-pink-400">Praise</dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 mt-1">
          Extended eulogy of a party leader (living or deceased) that does not advance an argument
          about policy. Panegyric language, honorific titles, invocation of legacy.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 rounded p-4">
        <dt class="font-semibold text-orange-700 dark:text-orange-400">Attack</dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 mt-1">
          Personal or ad-hominem criticism of an individual, party, or government that does not
          engage with policy substance. Sarcasm, insinuations, character comment.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 rounded p-4">
        <dt class="font-semibold text-red-700 dark:text-red-400">Uproar / Disruption</dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 mt-1">
          Overlapping speech, sloganeering, well-based protest, chair-thumping without a
          recognised speaker holding the floor.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 rounded p-4">
        <dt class="font-semibold text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-400">Reading statement</dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 mt-1">
          Verbatim reading of a prepared statement, gazette, or bill text without extemporaneous
          engagement. Statement of objects and reasons.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
      This rubric is a draft. It will be revised in public before we accept community-contributed
      corrections. Precision here matters — misclassifying substantive engagement as "praise" is
      a form of censorship; misclassifying rhetoric as "substantive" is flattery.
    </p>

    <h2>Confidence and corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Every derived field carries a confidence score. Segments below a threshold are flagged for
      human review. Anyone can inspect our raw output on GitHub and file a pull request with a
      correction; every accepted correction re-runs the sitting's aggregates.
    </p>

    <h2>What we do not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>We do not editorialise. There are no "verdicts" on speakers or parties.</li>
      <li>We do not modify the source video. Deep-links point to the assembly's own upload.</li>
      <li>We do not accept advertising, donations, or funding from political parties.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>
